<h2>Standard Sizes &amp; Dimensions — The Complete Reference Chart</h2>
<p>One of the most common crockery unit mistakes is getting the size wrong — either too shallow to hold full dinner plates, too narrow to house a complete set, or too tall for the ceiling height. This section gives you every critical dimension you need before commissioning or purchasing.</p>
<p>📏</p>
<p>350–450mm</p>
<p>Minimum Depth</p>
<p>Essential to accommodate dinner plates (250–300mm diameter). 350mm is absolute minimum. 400–450mm allows comfortable access and back-to-front stacking.</p>
<p>↔️</p>
<p>900–1800mm</p>
<p>Width Range</p>
<p>900mm is a small single unit. 1200mm fits 1–2 crockery sets. 1500–1800mm is ideal for households with 3+ crockery sets and glassware collections.</p>
<p>↕️</p>
<p>1800–2400mm</p>
<p>Standard Height</p>
<p>1800mm is floor-standing standard. 2100mm is ceiling-integrated for 9ft rooms. 2400mm for 10ft rooms with floor-to-ceiling treatment.</p>
<p>🍽️</p>
<p>300–350mm</p>
<p>Shelf Spacing (Plates)</p>
<p>Minimum shelf-to-shelf height for stacked dinner plates plus comfortable hand access. Never design shelves below 300mm for crockery storage.</p>
<p>🥂</p>
<p>250–320mm</p>
<p>Shelf Spacing (Glassware)</p>
<p>Wine glasses need 250mm minimum. Champagne flutes and tall glasses need up to 320mm. Design one shelf specifically for tall glassware storage.</p>
<p>🍵</p>
<p>200–250mm</p>
<p>Shelf Spacing (Mugs)</p>
<p>Standard mug height is 90–100mm. 200mm allows two-tier hanging mug storage with hooks. 250mm for oversized mugs and cups with large handles.</p>
<p>🪟</p>
<p>600–900mm</p>
<p>Glass Door Height</p>
<p>Each glass door panel should be 600–900mm tall for comfortable opening and visual proportion. Taller single doors require soft-close hinges with greater weight capacity.</p>
<p>🗄️</p>
<p>400–500mm</p>
<p>Base Cabinet Height</p>
<p>Lower closed storage section. 400–450mm is standard for linens and rarely-used sets. Must accommodate stacked pressure cookers if used as kitchen overflow storage.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>🎯 The Golden Sizing Rule for Indian Homes</strong> The most common sizing mistake is making the unit too shallow (under 350mm) to actually hold full dinner plates without the rims catching on the door frame when closing. Always confirm that your plates' full diameter + 30mm clearance fits within the cabinet depth before finalising.</div>
