<h2>Room-by-Room Placement Guide</h2>
<p>Where you place the crockery unit is as important as what it looks like. The wrong placement creates awkward traffic patterns, insufficient lighting, and visual imbalance. Each placement scenario below comes with specific design recommendations.</p>
<p>🍽️ Dedicated Dining Room Ideal Placement</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>Best Placement</strong>On the wall opposite the dining table's head, or on the longest uninterrupted wall of the dining room. The crockery unit should be the focal element of the dining room — framed by the room's architecture.</div>
<p>Design Recommendations</p>
<ul>
  <li>Full-height unit to use ceiling height fully</li>
  <li>Glass upper doors to display premium pieces</li>
  <li>Interior lighting at 2700K warm white</li>
  <li>Countertop at 850mm height as serving surface</li>
  <li>Match hardware and finish to dining table</li>
</ul>
<p>Common Mistakes Here</p>
<ul>
  <li>Unit too small — looks underpowered in the room</li>
  <li>Placed on a wall that gets direct sunlight — fades finishes and crockery</li>
  <li>No countertop for serving — purely display and storage</li>
  <li>Hardware mismatched with dining chair legs or light fitting</li>
</ul>
<p>🛋️ Open-Plan Living-Dining Most Common in Apartments</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>Placement Challenge</strong>In open-plan spaces, the crockery unit must work from multiple viewing angles — it is seen from the living area, the dining table, and the kitchen. It must be cohesive with all three zones.</div>
<p>Design Recommendations</p>
<ul>
  <li>Use the unit as a visual divider between dining and living zones</li>
  <li>A lower unit (900–1100mm) with display top creates separation without blocking light</li>
  <li>Finish should be consistent with both the kitchen palette and the living room palette</li>
  <li>Avoid units that sit flat against a wall — open a 3D space instead</li>
</ul>
<p>Space-Saving Strategies</p>
<ul>
  <li>Built-in unit on the wall between kitchen and dining saves floor space</li>
  <li>Peninsula-style unit doubles as bar counter and crockery storage</li>
  <li>Wall-mounted floating unit keeps floor area fully open — critical in smaller apartments</li>
  <li>Sliding glass doors instead of swinging doors save 300–400mm of clearance space</li>
</ul>
<p>🍳 Adjacent to Modular Kitchen Practical &amp; Space-Efficient</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>Why This Works</strong>A crockery unit adjacent to or integrated with the kitchen eliminates the distance between storage and serving — the most practical placement for daily-use crockery. Premium kitchens in 2026 increasingly include a dedicated crockery section as part of the kitchen design brief.</div>
<p>Integration Approaches</p>
<ul>
  <li>Extend the kitchen cabinetry into a crockery section with different door finish</li>
  <li>A tall unit at the kitchen entry creates a transition zone</li>
  <li>Match kitchen countertop material to crockery unit countertop for visual continuity</li>
  <li>Dedicated cutlery drawer inserts at counter height for daily-use silverware</li>
</ul>
<p>What to Specify</p>
<ul>
  <li>Moisture-resistant board — kitchen proximity increases humidity</li>
  <li>Soft-close hinges and drawers for daily heavy use</li>
  <li>Glass doors to distinguish from kitchen storage visually</li>
  <li>Interior LED lighting with motion sensor activation</li>
</ul>
<p>🏠 Living Room Feature Wall Showpiece Placement</p>
<div class="callout"><span class="callout__title">Note</span><strong>When This Works</strong>In homes without a dedicated dining room, a styled crockery display on the living room feature wall — integrated with a TV unit, bookshelves, or wall panelling — functions as both a statement piece and a practical storage solution.</div>
<p>Design Approach</p>
<ul>
  <li>Integrated into wall unit spanning full wall width</li>
  <li>Upper display section — open or glass-fronted for display pieces</li>
  <li>Lower section for daily crockery storage behind solid doors</li>
  <li>Style as a curated display — not purely functional storage</li>
</ul>
<p>Styling Principles</p>
<ul>
  <li>Display only your best pieces — not everyday chipped mugs</li>
  <li>Mix crockery with books, plants, and objets d'art for visual depth</li>
  <li>Backlight the display section at 2700K warm white</li>
  <li>Use a consistent colour palette in displayed items</li>
</ul>
<p>09</p>
<h2>Internal Layout &amp; Organisation — Designing the Inside</h2>
<p>The interior design of a crockery unit is where functionality is made or broken. Beautiful exterior design means nothing if the internal layout makes accessing your daily dinner plates a 5-step process.</p>
<ul>
  <li>✓<strong>Plate grooves on shelves.</strong> Horizontal wooden or metal grooves machined into shelf surfaces hold plates upright at a slight angle — visible for selection, safe against toppling, and dramatically more accessible than stacked horizontal piles. Standard groove pitch: 25mm spacing for standard plates.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Dedicated glassware section with non-slip shelf liner.</strong> A single shelf with increased height (300–350mm) and foam or silicone liner prevents glasses from sliding and chipping against each other. Position this shelf at mid-height for comfortable access.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Pull-out drawers for cutlery.</strong> Built-in cutlery drawer inserts in a full-extension soft-close drawer at counter height are the most practical way to store silverware — accessed without bending, organised at a glance, and protected from scratching against each other.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Linen drawer for table covers.</strong> A shallow wide drawer (150–180mm deep) for tablecloths, napkins, and placemats. Fold them flat rather than rolling — they stay unwrinkled and are immediately accessible before dinner setup.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Pull-out carcase sections for heavy items.</strong> Full-extension pull-out trays (not just shelves) for heavy items like pressure cookers, soup tureens, or serving bowls eliminate the need to remove everything to access items at the back.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Upper display section with adjustable shelves.</strong> Adjustable shelf pin holes every 32mm allow shelves to be repositioned as your collection changes. Fixed shelves are the enemy of a crockery collection that evolves over time.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Mug hooks on underside of shelves.</strong> Stainless steel or brass mug hooks mounted on the underside of a fixed shelf display mugs hanging by handle while keeping the shelf surface free for other items. Fits 6–10 mugs in the space typically used by 2.</li>
  <li>✓<strong>Wine rack integration.</strong> A diagonal wine bottle rack section — either a dedicated module or a 300–400mm section within the base cabinet — stores 6–12 bottles horizontally. More common in 2026 as Indian homeowners increasingly stock wine and host dinner parties.</li>
</ul>
<p>""") # ── PART 7: Features + Lighting + Glass ────────────────────────────────── parts.append(r"""</p>
<p>10</p>
<h2>Must-Have Features for Your Crockery Unit</h2>
<p>These are the features that separate a crockery unit that serves you well for 20 years from one that frustrates you daily after 6 months. Specify these when commissioning or evaluate them when purchasing a factory-made unit.</p>
<p>🔇</p>
<h4>Soft-Close Hinges &amp; Drawer Slides</h4>
<p>Every hinge and drawer should have hydraulic soft-close mechanism. This prevents slamming (which chips crockery through vibration), extends the life of the unit structure, and is now a baseline expectation in any unit above ₹25,000. Specify Hettich, Hafele, Grass, or Blum brand hardware — not generic.</p>
<p>NON-NEGOTIABLE</p>
<p>💡</p>
<h4>Interior LED Lighting</h4>
<p>LED strip lights on the underside of each shelf, or recessed puck lights at the top of each section. Motion-sensor activated is the premium specification — the light comes on when you open the door. 2700K warm white is the correct colour temperature for crockery display.</p>
<p>HIGHLY RECOMMENDED</p>
<p>🌬️</p>
<h4>Ventilation / Back Panel Gaps</h4>
<p>Fully sealed cabinets without ventilation develop musty smells — particularly in the base closed section. Specify a 15–20mm gap between the back panel and the wall, or laser-cut ventilation holes in the back panel. Critical for any unit stored in humid environments.</p>
<p>🪟</p>
<h4>Tempered Glass Doors</h4>
<p>All glass panels must be tempered (toughened) — not float glass. Tempered glass is 4–5x stronger, and if it does break, it shatters into small rounded pebbles rather than sharp shards. Minimum 4mm thickness for cabinet doors. 6mm for larger door panels above 600mm width.</p>
<p>SAFETY ESSENTIAL</p>
<p>⚓</p>
<h4>Wall Anchoring &amp; Anti-Tip</h4>
<p>Any full-height unit must be anchored to the wall at the top. An unanchored 2-metre crockery unit full of heavy dinnerware is a serious seismic and accidental-pull tip hazard. Specify a concealed L-bracket or wall cleat in the installation brief.</p>
<p>🛡️</p>
<h4>Waterproof Back Panel</h4>
<p>The back panel — typically 6mm or 9mm board — should be sealed with a waterproof laminate or acrylic paint on both sides. This prevents moisture absorption through the wall and eliminates the primary source of interior cabinet musty odour.</p>
<p>🔩</p>
<h4>Adjustable Shelf Pins</h4>
<p>All shelves in the upper display section should be adjustable on 32mm pin-hole systems. This allows reorganisation as your crockery collection evolves. Fixed shelves in display sections are the most commonly complained-about design limitation in existing crockery units.</p>
<p>🧲</p>
<h4>Magnetic Door Catches or Soft-Close Mechanism</h4>
<p>Glass doors that rattle or swing open on their own are the most common complaint about lower-quality crockery units. Specify magnetic touch-latches or soft-close hinge mechanisms that hold doors securely closed without a visible latch or knob.</p>
<p>📐</p>
<h4>Scribed Cornice &amp; Plinth</h4>
<p>A properly fitted crockery unit has a cornice (top detail piece) that meets the ceiling perfectly — scribed to follow any ceiling irregularities — and a plinth (base piece) that conceals the floor gap. Without these, even expensive units look unfinished and furniture-like rather than architectural.</p>
<p>11</p>
<h2>Crockery Unit Lighting — Transforming Display Into Drama</h2>
<p>Lighting is the single most impactful upgrade available for a crockery unit — it transforms a storage cabinet into a display case, makes crockery look dramatically more beautiful, and adds a warm ambient glow to the dining room even when the main lights are off. Yet it is one of the most commonly skipped specifications in Indian homes.</p>
<p>LED Strip Undershelf</p>
<p>Most practical and cost-effective option. LED strips (5W/m, 2700K) mounted on the underside of each shelf illuminate the shelf below. Channel-mounted aluminium profile with diffuser gives a clean, linear light source. Motion sensor option available.</p>
<p>Puck Spotlights</p>
<p>Small recessed or surface-mounted LED pucks (3–5W each) at the top of each section. Creates focused downlighting that brings out glassware sparkle particularly well. Best for sections housing crystal and premium glassware.</p>
<p>Backlit Panel</p>
<p>LED strip mounted behind the back panel with a frosted acrylic diffuser creating an even glowing panel. The crockery silhouettes against a glowing background — most dramatic effect. Best for sections with consistent display pieces.</p>
<p>Cove Uplight</p>
<p>LED strip mounted at the top rear of a cornice or pelmet casts light upward onto the ceiling above the unit. Creates a floating, glowing effect around the top of the unit. Best in combination with interior shelf lighting.</p>
<p>Magnetic Track Spots</p>
<p>Where the crockery unit is set into a ceiling niche or alcove, magnetic track spots in the ceiling provide directional lighting that can be aimed precisely at display sections. Most flexible option for villa dining rooms.</p>
<p>Motion Sensor</p>
<p>Add a PIR motion sensor to any internal lighting circuit. The lights activate when the door opens and shut off after 30–60 seconds. Eliminates the need to remember to turn display lighting on or off. Standard feature in premium specifications.</p>
<div class="callout callout--tip"><span class="callout__title">Tip</span><strong>The One Kelvin Rule for Crockery Lighting</strong><p>Always use 2700K warm white for crockery display lighting — never 4000K or 6500K. Warm light makes white crockery glow cream and ivory, makes crystal sparkle with golden fire, and gives the entire dining room a warm, welcoming quality. Cool white light on crockery makes it look clinical and cheap regardless of how expensive the pieces actually are.</div>
<p>12</p>
<h2>Glass Door Options — Choosing the Right Glass for Your Unit</h2>
<p>The glass panel in a crockery unit door is not merely a window — it defines the unit's personality, its visual weight, and how dramatically it displays the contents. There are now more options available than most Indian homeowners realise.</p>
<p>🪟</p>
<h4>Clear Tempered Glass</h4>
<p>Maximum visibility for displayed crockery. Every piece on display is fully visible at all times — which means every piece must be display-worthy. Best for premium crockery collections that are consistently well-organised and curated. 4mm minimum thickness.</p>
<p>🌫️</p>
<h4>Frosted / Acid-Etched Glass</h4>
<p>Translucent but not transparent — contents are visible as soft silhouettes. Hides minor crockery disorganisation. Allows interior lighting to glow through beautifully without showing every individual piece. Best for practical daily-use sections.</p>
<p>⬜</p>
<h4>Fluted / Reeded Glass</h4>
<p>Vertically ribbed glass — the most popular crockery unit glass choice in 2025–2026 India. Partially obscures contents while creating a beautiful vertical rhythm on the door face. Diffuses interior lighting into a dramatic warm glow. Available in clear, smoked, and bronze tints.</p>
<p>TRENDING</p>
<p>🌑</p>
<h4>Smoked / Bronze Tinted Glass</h4>
<p>Slightly tinted clear glass — reduces visibility by 40–60% while maintaining the transparency of glass. Contents are visible but not obviously so. Creates a jewel-box quality — the interior is revealed gradually as you approach. Very popular in dark-finish luxury units.</p>
<p>✨</p>
<h4>Antique Mirror Glass</h4>
<p>Aged, slightly distressed mirror glass as door panels — reflects the room while giving a vintage, layered quality to the display. Adds depth to the space and works beautifully in heritage, maximalist, and transitional interiors. The light from inside glows around the mirror backing.</p>
<p>💎</p>
<h4>Seedy / Bubble Glass</h4>
<p>Handblown-style glass with intentional air bubbles and texture — an artisanal quality that cannot be replicated industrially. Used in premium solid wood cabinets to complement the handcrafted quality of the woodwork. Expensive but genuinely unique.</p>
<p>ARTISAN</p>
