<h2>10 Costly Crockery Unit Mistakes — And How to Fix Them</h2>
<p>These are the ten errors that homeowners consistently discover — after installation — when it is expensive to correct. Read these before you commission or purchase.</p>
<p>1</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Making the unit too shallow — under 350mm depth — so dinner plates cannot be accessed without catching the rim on the door frame.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Specify minimum 380mm clear internal depth. Measure your largest plate, add 80mm clearance, and confirm this fits within the cabinet with the door closed.</p>
<p>2</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Using standard particle board or basic MDF in a coastal or humid city like Mumbai or Chennai — the unit swells, warps, and develops musty smells within 2–3 years.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Specify HDHMR board with PU-sealed all edges and a waterproof back panel treatment. This adds ₹5,000–10,000 to cost but extends unit life by 15–20 years in coastal conditions.</p>
<p>3</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>No interior lighting specified — then finding that without the lights on, the unit is essentially a dark box where you cannot tell a white plate from a cream one.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Retrofit LED strip lighting is possible — aluminium channel strips with adhesive backing can be added to an existing unit. Cost: ₹2,500–6,000. Requires an electrician to add a switch or motion sensor.</p>
<p>4</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Cheap generic hinges that fail within 18 months — glass doors start drooping, misaligning, and slamming rather than closing softly.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Replace with Hettich or Blum soft-close hinges. A carpenter can replace hinges in an existing unit for ₹2,000–4,000 labour plus hardware cost. Prevent this by specifying brand hardware upfront.</p>
<p>5</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Placing the unit on a wall that receives direct afternoon sunlight — causing UV fading on the cabinet finish and any displayed crockery within 2–3 years.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Use UV-filtering glass on any windows facing the unit, or reposition the unit to a wall not in direct sun. Apply a UV-protective topcoat to the cabinet finish if relocation is not possible.</p>
<p>6</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>No wall anchoring — a tall crockery unit fully loaded with heavy crockery, pulled forward by a child, can tip with catastrophic consequences.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Install a concealed L-bracket or wall cleat at the top rear of the unit, fixed into wall studs or with chemical anchors. This costs ₹500–2,000 to retrofit and takes 30 minutes with proper tools.</p>
<p>7</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>All shelves at the same height — making the unit unable to accommodate tall items like water jugs, champagne flutes, or decorative vases.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>If shelves are fixed, one shelf can be removed and repositioned with new pin holes drilled at appropriate heights by a carpenter. Cost: ₹1,500–3,000. Prevent this with adjustable shelves from the outset.</p>
<p>8</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Choosing a unit size based on what fits the space — rather than what holds your actual crockery collection — resulting in a unit that is full immediately and cannot grow.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Before specifying, count and measure every set of crockery you own. Add 30% for the sets you will acquire in the next 5 years. This number determines the minimum storage volume the unit must provide.</p>
<p>9</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>Selecting a finish in a showroom under display lighting — then finding it looks completely different in the actual dining room light conditions of the home.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Always take a finish sample (laminate chip, veneer sample, or painted chip) home and evaluate it under both daylight and artificial evening light in the actual room before finalising.</p>
<p>10</p>
<p><strong>The Mistake</strong></p>
<p>No ventilation in the closed base section — within months, a musty, enclosed smell develops and transfers to stored linens and accessories.</p>
<p><strong>The Fix</strong></p>
<p>Drill a 15mm hole with a Forstner bit behind the plinth of each base cabinet section, or install a small decorative louvred grill in the side panel. Takes 30 minutes to retrofit and completely resolves the problem.</p>
<p>18</p>
